1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
London, England. A cosmopolitan city. Home to over seven million people.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Traffic congestion has long been a problem in the metropolis.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:16,000
150 years ago, London needed a solution to its transport problems.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
The solution was to give the world its first underground railway system.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
The history of the London Underground, next on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:48,849 --> 00:00:53,640
Rush hour at Kings Cross Station, one of central London's busiest underground stations.

7
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:59,000
Six different tube lines meet here, and it also serves a major railroad station,

8
00:00:59,001 --> 00:01:02,440
with 67 million passengers passing through each year.

9
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,900
Few realised the role it played in giving London its underground.

10
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:10,520
The first steam railways had begun in 1825,

11
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:15,800
and 25 years later, King's Cross was a terminus for steam trains arriving in London.

12
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:20,360
But the railroads were not permitted to expand any further into the capital.

13
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:25,620
This meant that the 750,000 workers who arrived at the railroads each day

14
00:01:25,621 --> 00:01:28,520
then had to find a way to get to work in the heart of the city.

15
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,400
This huge burden of traffic inevitably led to gridlock.

16
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,400
London of the 1850s was one of the world's largest cities.

17
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:40,880
It was a massively growing industrial city and a port,

18
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,800
the hub of the start of the British Empire.

19
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:47,200
Interestingly enough, it was a very dense and crowded city.

20
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:50,400
It was still largely built on its medieval street pattern.

21
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:53,360
And it meant that people basically jostled together.

22
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:55,720
People lived very close to their work.

23
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:58,720
And it was a very crowded city full of cobbled streets,

24
00:01:58,721 --> 00:02:00,820
gas lights and horse-drawn vehicles.

25
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:05,760
150 years ago, London was grinding to a halt,

26
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,860
and it needed an ambitious solution

27
00:02:07,861 --> 00:02:10,440
to link the main railroad stations to the city centre.

28
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:14,900
There was only one way to go, and that was underground.

29
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:17,680
It's important to remember that for most people,

30
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:19,380
the thought of actually going underground

31
00:02:19,381 --> 00:02:21,220
and travelling at speed underground

32
00:02:21,221 --> 00:02:23,580
was actually a very novel concept.

33
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:28,140
Many Victorian writers saw death and doom and destruction

34
00:02:28,141 --> 00:02:30,680
to people who actually travelled on underground railways.

35
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:33,720
Newspapers such as The Times

36
00:02:33,721 --> 00:02:36,860
actually ran quite a fierce campaign against the railway

37
00:02:36,861 --> 00:02:38,940
saying that even if it worked technically,

38
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:41,600
it would never pay because people would never use it.

39
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,740
They were speculating about underground passages

40
00:02:44,741 --> 00:02:47,660
soaked with sewage drippings and infested by rats.

41
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:52,000
Going underground was the vision of one man, Charles Pearson.

42
00:02:52,001 --> 00:02:56,180
He persuaded Londoners that they needed a transport network beneath the streets.

43
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:00,100
He knew that if people could get in and out of the city easily,

44
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:04,240
they could live further away and the slums in London would become a thing of the past.

45
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:08,760
Charles Pearson was a lawyer associated with the City of London

46
00:03:08,761 --> 00:03:14,380
and he was the person who really first pressed for the building of the Metropolitan Railway,

47
00:03:14,381 --> 00:03:17,480
which was the world's first underground steam railway.

48
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:23,260
And Pearson basically came up with the idea of digging a trench down the middle of the road,

49
00:03:23,261 --> 00:03:27,340
of roofing it over and running his railway in the bottom of the trench.

50
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:31,520
The Metropolitan Railway Company broke ground in 1860.

51
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:34,800
The building method used was called cut and cover

52
00:03:34,801 --> 00:03:37,380
and was a very expensive engineering project.

53
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:41,100
It involved digging tunnels which were over 20 foot wide,

54
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:46,040
laying two tracks side by side and then bricking these over to replace the road.

55
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:51,220
This project took three years to complete and caused havoc to homes and businesses.

56
00:03:51,221 --> 00:03:56,220
The new tunnels were very shallow and ran only feet below the road surface.

57
00:03:56,221 --> 00:04:00,220
An example of this can still be seen in the Notting Hill district of London.

58
00:04:00,221 --> 00:04:05,220
There are two dummy houses, numbers 23 and 24 Leinster Gardens,

59
00:04:05,221 --> 00:04:10,220
which are identical to all the other houses in the street, except for one thing.

60
00:04:10,221 --> 00:04:16,220
Their frontage is merely a wall, five foot thick, complete with painted windows and front doors.

61
00:04:16,221 --> 00:04:21,280
They were built to fill the gap left in the row of houses when the line was built through the street.

62
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:28,320
The Metropolitan Line opened in January 1863,

63
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:32,460
and it basically connected the three major rail terminals at Paddington,

64
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:36,440
at Euston, and at King's Cross with the centre of the city at Farringdon.

65
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:42,280
It was not conceived as a steam railway.

66
00:04:42,281 --> 00:04:50,800
The initial suggestion by the Metropolitan Railway's engineer, John Fowler, had been to have the new underground railway running on compressed air.

67
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:59,320
The idea was that trains would be blown through tunnels and sucked back by air generated in great compressors at each end of the line.

68
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:06,360
But the major flaw in this system was that the compressed air leaked continuously through the tunnel joints.

69
00:05:06,361 --> 00:05:13,000
With the failure of the system, the Metropolitan Railway was forced to borrow steam locomotives

70
00:05:13,001 --> 00:05:18,540
and rolling stock from the Great Western Railway in order to start its service.

71
00:05:18,541 --> 00:05:23,720
Although it's just a quaint tourist attraction today, it was a revolutionary idea and an

72
00:05:23,721 --> 00:05:25,300
immediate success.

73
00:05:25,301 --> 00:05:28,600
It carried 40,000 passengers on its first day.

74
00:05:28,601 --> 00:05:33,880
The timetable ran between 8am and 8pm with the train every 15 minutes.

75
00:05:33,881 --> 00:05:37,320
The time for the three and three quarter mile journey from Paddington to Farringdon was

76
00:05:37,321 --> 00:05:41,140
18 minutes, stopping at all stations.

77
00:05:41,141 --> 00:05:44,660
Not much slower than the journey time today.

78
00:05:44,661 --> 00:05:48,980
This newly built railway also had one oddity when it started.

79
00:05:48,981 --> 00:05:56,140
When the Metropolitan Railway was first opened, it had been constructed with both seven foot

80
00:05:56,141 --> 00:06:01,200
gauge which was Brunel's Great Western Railway broad gauge because it met and on with the

81
00:06:01,201 --> 00:06:05,480
Great Western Railway at the Paddington end and it had a third rail added which

82
00:06:05,481 --> 00:06:10,380
also created a four foot eight and a half inch gauge track that Stevenson's

83
00:06:10,381 --> 00:06:15,480
gauge. The gauge is the distance between the two running tracks this odd twin

84
00:06:15,481 --> 00:06:19,440
gauge arrangement ended when the two companies the Metropolitan Railway and

85
00:06:19,441 --> 00:06:23,700
the Great Western fell out with each other the Metropolitan went back to

86
00:06:23,701 --> 00:06:29,940
using Stevenson's gauge and this is still used by most railways today. Without the

87
00:06:29,941 --> 00:06:34,340
locomotives from the Great Western Railway, the Metropolitan was forced into supplying

88
00:06:34,341 --> 00:06:35,754
their own rolling stock.

89
00:06:35,755 --> 00:06:39,700
And for this, they turned to the engineering company Bayer Peacock.

90
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:42,425
The engine was a 440 tank engine.

91
00:06:42,426 --> 00:06:46,000
440 is the wheel arrangement also used on the standard

92
00:06:46,001 --> 00:06:47,857
American engines of the time.

93
00:06:47,858 --> 00:06:51,700
And it had fairly large driving wheels as a passenger engine

94
00:06:51,701 --> 00:06:54,060
at the time. Woodham, a very, very handsome beast.

95
00:06:54,061 --> 00:06:55,760
Very successful, used throughout the

96
00:06:55,761 --> 00:06:59,240
whole 40-year period of steam on the Metropolitan Railway.

97
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:02,860
The engines weighed about 46 tonnes in running order.

98
00:07:03,580 --> 00:07:07,120
They could carry approximately 1,000 gallons of water in their side tanks,

99
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:10,720
and the coupled driving wheels were 5 feet 9 inches in diameter,

100
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:13,220
driven by two 17-inch cylinders.

101
00:07:14,580 --> 00:07:18,340
These are the original coaches which were used in London in the 19th century.

102
00:07:19,140 --> 00:07:22,100
They have been carefully restored by a railway preservation society,

103
00:07:22,101 --> 00:07:25,620
and each year they are exhibited for enthusiasts to see.

104
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:30,760
The Dreadnoughts, as these carriages were known, were divided according to class.

105
00:07:32,760 --> 00:07:36,760
The fares charged to passengers on the Metropolitan Railway

106
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:39,760
would have been according to the class they travelled in.

107
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:44,760
First class would have been used by top city people travelling to their offices

108
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:47,760
from their fashionable West London homes.

109
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:52,760
Second class would have been used probably by the middle level of management, clerks and so on.

110
00:07:52,761 --> 00:07:55,880
and third-class anybody else who needed to travel.

111
00:07:56,480 --> 00:07:58,680
And they would have travelled fairly uncomfortably

112
00:07:58,681 --> 00:08:01,160
and in very cramped conditions on wooden boards.

113
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:06,680
The success of the Metropolitan encouraged other companies to start up,

114
00:08:06,681 --> 00:08:12,200
and in 1874, London's second Underground Railway began running along the Thames Embankment.

115
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:15,620
This was the Metropolitan and District Railway.

116
00:08:16,540 --> 00:08:20,800
By 1884, a complete circular Underground Railroad had been constructed

117
00:08:20,801 --> 00:08:24,380
as a joint venture by the Metropolitan and the District Railways.

118
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:28,340
They called it the Circle Line, and it's still in use today,

119
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:33,260
carrying passengers in its shallow tunnels just feet beneath Big Ben and the Tower of London.

120
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:37,640
But the new century would bring a new technology.

121
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:41,440
The use of steam locomotives on underground railways,

122
00:08:41,540 --> 00:08:45,280
particularly on the Metropolitan Railway and on the District Railway and some others,

123
00:08:45,740 --> 00:08:48,480
became increasingly problematical as time went on.

124
00:08:48,481 --> 00:08:53,300
The intensity of the service increased, the intervals between the trains shortened,

125
00:08:54,120 --> 00:09:00,580
and passengers were increasingly aggravated by the terrible pollution conditions which existed down there.

126
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:08,080
At the same time, there were technological advances which were occurring in America, for example,

127
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:10,820
and in Germany particularly, in electric traction.

128
00:09:11,860 --> 00:09:15,040
With electrification, the underground was able to expand.

129
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:18,820
However, the extremely disruptive cut-and-cover method

130
00:09:18,821 --> 00:09:21,240
no longer seemed an option for building the railways.

131
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:24,220
London couldn't cope with the chaos to its roads,

132
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:27,140
and so a new type of underground construction was needed.

133
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:30,980
This was going to take the builders deep into the London clay.

134
00:09:32,740 --> 00:09:35,680
This is a standard London underground running tunnel,

135
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:38,400
manufactured out of cast-iron lining rings.

136
00:09:38,401 --> 00:09:41,760
And this slightly larger section of tunnel that we're standing in

137
00:09:41,761 --> 00:09:43,280
is called a shield chamber.

138
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:45,380
This is where the Great Head shield,

139
00:09:45,760 --> 00:09:48,900
the device used to help build London's underground railways,

140
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:51,620
was constructed and then dismantled.

141
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:56,160
A South African engineer called James Henry Great Head

142
00:09:56,161 --> 00:09:59,320
invented a thing called a tunnelling shield.

143
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:02,500
It was a kind of iron cylinder with a wall

144
00:10:02,501 --> 00:10:06,440
in which miners could actually work in safety in all the soft ground.

145
00:10:07,200 --> 00:10:09,580
The earth would be dug out in front of the tunnelling shield

146
00:10:09,581 --> 00:10:12,060
and when a hole large enough had been constructed,

147
00:10:12,061 --> 00:10:14,440
the device could be pushed forward.

148
00:10:15,080 --> 00:10:17,680
The tunnel lining could then be erected behind the shield

149
00:10:17,681 --> 00:10:20,280
to hold the ground up and then the process repeated.

150
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:24,480
The Great Head Tunnelling Shield allowed the London Underground

151
00:10:24,481 --> 00:10:27,160
to expand into a deep-level railway network.

152
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:31,000
The shield was the single most important engineering invention

153
00:10:31,001 --> 00:10:32,560
used to create the underground.

154
00:10:33,620 --> 00:10:36,300
The longest journey you can make on the London Underground

155
00:10:36,301 --> 00:10:39,540
without changing trains is over 34 miles.

156
00:10:39,541 --> 00:10:46,540
It's on the Central Line, between West Reislit and Epping.

157
00:10:46,541 --> 00:10:52,540
London at the end of the 19th century was filled with exciting transport innovations.

158
00:10:52,541 --> 00:10:57,540
Permission had been granted to numerous railroad companies who were proposing deep-level underground routes.

159
00:10:57,541 --> 00:11:01,540
But the city and South London Railway was the first to make history.

160
00:11:01,541 --> 00:11:08,540
In 1890, it became the first electric underground railroad in the world.

161
00:11:08,541 --> 00:11:13,500
The decision to actually use electric traction was a really ambitious thing to do at the time,

162
00:11:13,860 --> 00:11:17,840
because most electric traction systems that were in use were tramways.

163
00:11:18,380 --> 00:11:22,220
Getting the electrical equipment small enough to actually run in a tunnel

164
00:11:22,221 --> 00:11:26,740
and powerful enough to move these quite large trains was a big challenge.

165
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:32,000
The only way they could do it was to put all the electrical equipment into a small locomotive.

166
00:11:32,620 --> 00:11:35,200
The power station for the line was down at Stockwell.

167
00:11:35,201 --> 00:11:38,200
The problems they had were that the distribution of electricity

168
00:11:38,201 --> 00:11:42,200
from the power station up and down the line was not very good.

169
00:11:42,201 --> 00:11:46,200
So as the locomotives got further and further away from the power station

170
00:11:46,201 --> 00:11:50,200
and started to approach the station in the city, up at King William Street,

171
00:11:50,201 --> 00:11:52,200
they often found that they were completely underpowered.

172
00:11:52,201 --> 00:11:55,200
And as the trains struggled to go up and down the hills,

173
00:11:55,201 --> 00:11:57,200
occasionally they would grind to a halt completely,

174
00:11:57,201 --> 00:12:01,200
and all the lights would come out, leaving the passengers stranded in darkness.

175
00:12:01,201 --> 00:12:08,200
The trains had technical problems and their carriages offered little passenger comfort.

176
00:12:08,201 --> 00:12:14,200
They were dubbed the Sardine Box Railway or Padded Cell because of their claustrophobic design.

177
00:12:14,201 --> 00:12:21,200
But the City and South London Railway proved that electricity was the only way forward for the Underground.

178
00:12:21,201 --> 00:12:26,200
Another electric powered tube company began operating in 1900.

179
00:12:26,201 --> 00:12:30,200
The Central London Railway was opened by Queen Victoria's son, the Prince of Wales.

180
00:12:30,201 --> 00:12:33,200
The line was built entirely in tunnels, ten feet in diameter,

181
00:12:33,201 --> 00:12:36,200
and since it ran across central London from east to west,

182
00:12:36,201 --> 00:12:40,200
there was never much fear that it would fail from lack of passengers.

183
00:12:40,201 --> 00:12:43,200
Today, the line is simply known as the Central Line

184
00:12:43,201 --> 00:12:49,200
and serves stations along its route including Oxford Circus, Bond Street and Hoban.

185
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:55,200
The Central London Railway was immediately popular with the travelling public.

186
00:12:55,201 --> 00:12:56,454
travelling public.

187
00:12:56,455 --> 00:13:01,260
Over 14 million passengers were carried by the end of the first year.

188
00:13:01,261 --> 00:13:06,570
The system was popular because it adopted an American idea of a flat rate fare.

189
00:13:06,571 --> 00:13:07,271
It also

190
00:13:07,272 --> 00:13:11,640
looked across the Atlantic for its new technology.

191
00:13:11,641 --> 00:13:16,320
The original central London electric locomotives were what they were called camelbacks because

192
00:13:16,321 --> 00:13:20,700
they had a cab in the middle and two sloping bonnets on either side, which were built in

193
00:13:20,701 --> 00:13:23,267
America by the General Electric Company.

194
00:13:23,268 --> 00:13:26,540
They were shipped to England in boxes and assembled

195
00:13:26,541 --> 00:13:27,241
in London.

196
00:13:27,242 --> 00:13:30,860
One of them was dropped off a barge into the River Thames and had to be fished

197
00:13:30,861 --> 00:13:33,400
out before they could build it.

198
00:13:33,401 --> 00:13:37,507
The passenger cars were over 45 feet long and weighed 14 tons.

199
00:13:37,508 --> 00:13:39,560
Gateman controlled iron-grilled

200
00:13:39,561 --> 00:13:42,903
gates at each end of the cars allowing passengers to board.

201
00:13:42,904 --> 00:13:44,660
The railway used direct current

202
00:13:44,661 --> 00:13:46,133
for its traction power.

203
00:13:46,134 --> 00:13:50,360
This electricity was picked up by the locomotive from a third rail

204
00:13:50,361 --> 00:13:52,560
positioned in the middle of the two running rails.

205
00:13:54,280 --> 00:13:57,300
When you want to start an electric motor driving a train,

206
00:13:57,880 --> 00:14:00,160
if you start it with all the power all at once,

207
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:03,000
it'll either go into a skid or the motor will blow up

208
00:14:03,001 --> 00:14:05,020
because it's had too much power when it's standing still.

209
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:07,100
So you have to apply the power gradually,

210
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:10,260
rather the same way as you change up your gears in the car.

211
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:14,040
And the way they do it is they put a grid of resistors

212
00:14:14,041 --> 00:14:15,280
in the circuit with the motor,

213
00:14:15,980 --> 00:14:18,300
and the resistance reduces the amount of current

214
00:14:18,301 --> 00:14:19,420
getting into the motor circuit.

215
00:14:19,421 --> 00:14:23,740
and as you want to accelerate you gradually step out the resistances one by one,

216
00:14:23,741 --> 00:14:28,460
you cut them out and if you listen you can hear that happening click click click click

217
00:14:28,461 --> 00:14:31,420
as the train accelerates out of the station.

218
00:14:31,421 --> 00:14:35,580
In 1900 London's population was fast approaching seven million

219
00:14:35,581 --> 00:14:40,860
and there was a heavy reliance on the tube to move these millions quickly and efficiently.

220
00:14:40,861 --> 00:14:44,860
But there was a problem with the locomotives.

221
00:14:44,861 --> 00:14:49,020
The time taken to uncouple the locomotive when you got to the end of the line

222
00:14:49,021 --> 00:14:52,180
and find another one to put on the other end of the train to return, to go back,

223
00:14:52,740 --> 00:14:55,360
was taking up a lot of time and reducing the service.

224
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:59,020
So they introduced what was called multiple unit control.

225
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:04,080
They achieved this by putting two of the motors at one end of the train

226
00:15:04,081 --> 00:15:07,080
and two at the other, underneath the passenger cars.

227
00:15:08,040 --> 00:15:12,040
The resistors and electrical equipment were put into a space above the motors.

228
00:15:13,460 --> 00:15:16,180
All the driver had to do when he wanted to change ends

229
00:15:16,181 --> 00:15:18,140
was to shut down the cab at one end,

230
00:15:18,141 --> 00:15:23,240
walk down the train, get in the cab at the other end, and he was ready to go.

231
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:27,300
Over the next few years, there was really a railway mania

232
00:15:27,301 --> 00:15:32,360
with many, many companies and organisations promoting railways,

233
00:15:32,480 --> 00:15:34,240
very few of which ever saw the light of day.

234
00:15:34,760 --> 00:15:38,140
But three did, forming part of today's Northern Line,

235
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:40,060
Bakaloo Line and Piccadilly Line,

236
00:15:40,680 --> 00:15:44,240
and all three had been given sanction by the government to go ahead,

237
00:15:44,241 --> 00:15:46,220
but all three were running into financial trouble.

238
00:15:46,221 --> 00:15:52,240
And that's when the American financier Charles Tyson Yerkes came to the rescue.

239
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:57,480
Charles Tyson Yerkes arrived in London with his wife and several of his mistresses,

240
00:15:58,020 --> 00:16:00,280
leaving behind in the US a shady reputation.

241
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:04,240
Questionable business practices in America led to a prison sentence

242
00:16:04,241 --> 00:16:06,500
for his misappropriation of government funds.

243
00:16:08,080 --> 00:16:11,180
Charles Tyson Yerkes is a fascinating character.

244
00:16:11,181 --> 00:16:15,860
To many people, he's the embodiment of the crooked Victorian businessman.

245
00:16:16,220 --> 00:16:21,320
But I think it's important to remember that, of course, he merely operated in the business climate of the day.

246
00:16:21,321 --> 00:16:28,820
He wasn't responsible for the first underground railway, but what he brought was basically access to several important things.

247
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:35,980
He brought access to American money to develop the very expensive tube railways being built in London at the turn of the century.

248
00:16:36,340 --> 00:16:40,520
And he brought access to American technology, particularly in the electrical field.

249
00:16:40,521 --> 00:16:45,520
By 1902, Yerkes had become the chairman of the newly formed

250
00:16:45,521 --> 00:16:48,520
Underground Electric Railways Company of London Limited

251
00:16:48,521 --> 00:16:50,480
with a capital of $4 million.

252
00:16:51,860 --> 00:16:55,180
Yerkes set about buying up the new electric underground lines.

253
00:16:56,260 --> 00:16:59,360
He also built a large power plant at Lots Road, Chelsea,

254
00:16:59,800 --> 00:17:03,620
which was an ideal location for being supplied with coal along the River Thames.

255
00:17:04,240 --> 00:17:08,260
He believed that if you supplied your own power, you increased your profits.

256
00:17:08,261 --> 00:17:14,660
The Lotz Road power plant, which is still clearly visible on the London skyline, was

257
00:17:14,661 --> 00:17:17,716
built to specifications to that of American powerhouses.

258
00:17:17,717 --> 00:17:19,680
It was one of the biggest generating

259
00:17:19,681 --> 00:17:24,980
stations in the world. It contained eight turbo generators running at 1,000 revs per

260
00:17:24,981 --> 00:17:29,720
minute, developing 65,000 horsepower.

261
00:17:29,721 --> 00:17:35,140
The opening of Yerkes' first tube lines in 1906 and the increasing electrification of

262
00:17:35,141 --> 00:17:37,180
of his subsurface railways.

263
00:17:37,260 --> 00:17:40,620
Increasingly, this felt the death knell of steam locomotives

264
00:17:40,700 --> 00:17:43,940
in use on the central area of London Underground.

265
00:17:44,020 --> 00:17:46,380
Progressively, they were forced to the suburbs,

266
00:17:46,460 --> 00:17:49,740
and eventually the entire network was electrically operated.

267
00:17:51,060 --> 00:17:53,020
When the Underground was first electrified,

268
00:17:53,100 --> 00:17:55,500
it was decided to use a four-rail system,

269
00:17:55,580 --> 00:17:58,340
which is almost unique in the world.

270
00:17:58,420 --> 00:18:02,780
The positive one is on the outside of the rails,

271
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:04,780
and then there's the one in the centre,

272
00:18:04,781 --> 00:18:05,820
which is the negative rail.

273
00:18:06,100 --> 00:18:09,720
That system was chosen with an insulated return

274
00:18:09,721 --> 00:18:12,560
because even in those early days of electrification,

275
00:18:13,160 --> 00:18:16,800
it was realised that if the return went through the running rails and through Earth,

276
00:18:17,080 --> 00:18:20,460
it could cause problems with corrosion of water pipes

277
00:18:20,461 --> 00:18:24,540
and other metal in the ground, including our own tunnel segments.

278
00:18:25,880 --> 00:18:28,120
Charles Yerkes had achieved his first goal

279
00:18:28,121 --> 00:18:31,380
by making an efficient, electrically-powered railway network.

280
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,240
The system he set up at the beginning of the century

281
00:18:34,241 --> 00:18:37,020
is still very much in place in the modern tube.

282
00:18:38,340 --> 00:18:42,500
But how to give the entire underground network a uniform identity

283
00:18:42,501 --> 00:18:44,520
would be Yerkes' next challenge.

284
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:48,180
Modern styling and improved design were to be the answer.

285
00:18:50,260 --> 00:18:54,360
Oxford Circus Station, at the centre of London's popular shopping district,

286
00:18:54,361 --> 00:18:56,620
is the busiest station on the underground.

287
00:18:58,100 --> 00:19:01,600
87.8 million people use the station every year.

288
00:19:04,240 --> 00:19:12,240
By 1905, Charles Tyson Yerkes had achieved his aim of electrifying the three deep-level railways beneath London.

289
00:19:12,241 --> 00:19:18,240
To create a uniform identity for his company, he employed a young architect, Leslie Green,

290
00:19:18,241 --> 00:19:21,240
to design stations that were distinctive and conspicuous.

291
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:28,240
The minute you see one of these stations, it says London Underground,

292
00:19:28,241 --> 00:19:33,240
because it uses the tiles, these maroon glazed tiles, always the same.

293
00:19:33,241 --> 00:19:36,240
You also have all those Edwardian obsessions...

294
00:19:36,241 --> 00:19:39,240
...with kind of an English variant on Art Nouveau.

295
00:19:39,241 --> 00:19:44,240
You've got craftsmanship and wood and no expense spared.

296
00:19:44,241 --> 00:19:47,240
So these were lavish stations in their time.

297
00:19:47,241 --> 00:19:52,240
And they've lasted the test of time extraordinarily well.

298
00:19:52,241 --> 00:19:54,240
The minute you walk into a Leslie Green station...

299
00:19:54,241 --> 00:19:57,240
...you know you're on the London Underground.

300
00:19:57,241 --> 00:20:01,240
They made something like 11 Leslie Green stations in two years...

301
00:20:01,241 --> 00:20:06,740
years between 1904 and 1906, which is probably why he died a year later, they say, of exhaustion

302
00:20:06,741 --> 00:20:11,680
aged 33. But he left behind an extraordinary heritage for London.

303
00:20:13,260 --> 00:20:16,544
Yerkes also didn't live to see his tube empire grow.

304
00:20:16,545 --> 00:20:18,880
He died in December 1905 in New York,

305
00:20:19,180 --> 00:20:23,664
and the following year he was replaced by another American, Albert Stanley.

306
00:20:23,665 --> 00:20:24,860
Stanley brought even

307
00:20:24,861 --> 00:20:29,500
more American influence onto the underground when he arrived from New Jersey and took over as general

308
00:20:29,501 --> 00:20:32,777
manager of the Underground Electric Railways Company.

309
00:20:32,778 --> 00:20:34,880
Albert Stanley had two outstanding

310
00:20:34,881 --> 00:20:35,581
achievements.

311
00:20:35,582 --> 00:20:39,660
Firstly, he bought the bus companies in London, the only mode of transport that

312
00:20:39,661 --> 00:20:44,354
made a profit. Secondly, he employed Frank Pick as his deputy in 1906.

313
00:20:44,355 --> 00:20:45,360
Pick became the

314
00:20:45,361 --> 00:20:48,674
man responsible for branding and styling on the Underground.

315
00:20:48,675 --> 00:20:50,220
This included rolling stock,

316
00:20:50,740 --> 00:20:55,280
upholstery, lighting, architecture and even graphic design.

317
00:20:55,281 --> 00:21:00,280
The Underground, when they first opened the new tube lines,

318
00:21:00,281 --> 00:21:06,280
had the names of the stations simply fired into the tiles at either end of the platform.

319
00:21:06,281 --> 00:21:11,280
One of the first changes they made, so that the station name was distinctive,

320
00:21:11,281 --> 00:21:19,280
was to have the name printed on an enamel plate with behind it a red symbol,

321
00:21:19,281 --> 00:21:25,340
so that the lettering is on white on a blue plate with a red disc behind it.

322
00:21:26,220 --> 00:21:30,180
The circle with a bar across it became a symbol of the underground company

323
00:21:30,181 --> 00:21:32,780
and remains its corporate logo even today.

324
00:21:33,560 --> 00:21:38,740
This logo reinforced the way the three deep-level tube lines were seen to be part of the same company.

325
00:21:39,420 --> 00:21:43,900
It was called the underground for the first time in 1908 and the name stuck.

326
00:21:44,600 --> 00:21:47,340
The newly branded company also modernised its stock

327
00:21:47,341 --> 00:21:49,640
as the modern tube continued to take shape.

328
00:21:50,300 --> 00:21:51,980
To increase the efficiency of the service,

329
00:21:52,460 --> 00:21:56,780
the underground had to find a way of getting people on and off the trains more quickly.

330
00:21:57,700 --> 00:22:02,220
They solved the problem by the 1920s by building trains

331
00:22:02,221 --> 00:22:04,120
which had sliding doors on the sides,

332
00:22:04,260 --> 00:22:06,400
and the tops of the doors followed the roof profile

333
00:22:06,401 --> 00:22:08,980
so that there was headroom for people to get on and off.

334
00:22:09,500 --> 00:22:11,740
And this stop became known as the standard stop

335
00:22:11,741 --> 00:22:15,160
because so much of it was built that it was practically standard

336
00:22:15,161 --> 00:22:16,980
across all of the Yerkes tubes.

337
00:22:17,340 --> 00:22:22,340
The underground had a modern identity and modern trains below ground.

338
00:22:22,341 --> 00:22:27,340
But now it was about to play a major role in the shaping of modern day London above ground.

339
00:22:27,341 --> 00:22:35,340
The underground lines had expanded and in 1908 one line came to the surface just to the north of London in open countryside.

340
00:22:35,341 --> 00:22:43,340
Within the next few years an entire new community, Hampstead, Garden, Suburb, developed around the base of the underground station.

341
00:22:43,341 --> 00:22:51,200
Albert Stanley and his fellow investors realised that there was a great profit to be made in the expansion and development of the London suburbs,

342
00:22:51,700 --> 00:22:54,520
as did their arch-rivals, the Metropolitan Railway.

343
00:22:55,040 --> 00:23:00,040
Together, the two companies expanded their networks beyond London, creating a new suburbia.

344
00:23:00,760 --> 00:23:08,800
The Metropolitan Railway had the smart idea of actually buying up land either side of the railway as it was developed out into the countryside,

345
00:23:08,801 --> 00:23:14,060
and then later using that land to develop their own housing estates.

346
00:23:14,280 --> 00:23:18,800
So they got the double benefit of not only the profits from the houses themselves,

347
00:23:19,060 --> 00:23:22,100
but also the profits from the people who bought those houses,

348
00:23:22,380 --> 00:23:24,540
then travelling in daily into London.

349
00:23:25,280 --> 00:23:29,360
The centre of London eventually stopped being the place where people lived and worked.

350
00:23:29,840 --> 00:23:32,220
The rapid growth in desirable suburban housing

351
00:23:32,221 --> 00:23:35,100
meant that the city centre was reinventing itself

352
00:23:35,101 --> 00:23:38,060
as the place to visit for entertainment and shopping.

353
00:23:38,800 --> 00:23:42,600
From very early on, the people who run the London transport system

354
00:23:42,601 --> 00:23:47,600
realised that what they were selling was not a transport system, but a city.

355
00:23:47,601 --> 00:23:49,600
See, if you're selling tickets on the Tube, you don't say,

356
00:23:49,601 --> 00:23:51,600
''Come on the Tube, it's great fun.''

357
00:23:51,601 --> 00:23:53,600
You say, ''Go to the theatre, it's great fun.''

358
00:23:53,601 --> 00:23:54,600
And go by Tube.

359
00:23:54,601 --> 00:23:56,600
Or you say, ''Go shopping in the department stores.''

360
00:23:56,601 --> 00:23:58,600
It's great, go by Tube.

361
00:24:00,600 --> 00:24:03,600
The underground network had expanded from the centre of London

362
00:24:03,601 --> 00:24:06,600
and out to the suburbs, reaching ever more people.

363
00:24:06,601 --> 00:24:09,600
But to make a profit, it needed even more passengers.

364
00:24:10,560 --> 00:24:14,600
Frank Pick, the assistant manager of the Underground, hit on a radical idea.

365
00:24:15,700 --> 00:24:21,680
A poster campaign to promote the tube, not just as a way of getting to work, but for leisure activities.

366
00:24:21,681 --> 00:24:27,820
By the 1920s and 30s, the Underground was producing a really large number of posters.

367
00:24:28,620 --> 00:24:34,820
And they had easily fulfilled their function of just publicizing the system and getting people to use the Underground.

368
00:24:34,821 --> 00:24:37,020
and they became much more than that.

369
00:24:37,220 --> 00:24:40,620
They became a way of actually promoting London.

370
00:24:41,620 --> 00:24:45,020
So they've sold the West End as the place to shop, the place to play,

371
00:24:45,220 --> 00:24:48,020
the place to drink, the place to flirt, the place to do all of those things.

372
00:24:48,220 --> 00:24:50,220
Consciously, posters saying,

373
00:24:50,420 --> 00:24:53,820
Come to the West End, it's great. Go to the theatre, it's wonderful.

374
00:24:54,020 --> 00:24:57,820
Go to Selfridges and spend your money and buy a two-bob ticket.

375
00:24:58,020 --> 00:25:00,620
That's what they sold, and we bought it.

376
00:25:00,820 --> 00:25:04,220
The marketing and poster campaigns exemplified the forward-thinking

377
00:25:04,221 --> 00:25:08,900
thinking and innovation of the underground, but in the engineering arena, older technology

378
00:25:08,901 --> 00:25:10,720
was still serving them well.

379
00:25:10,721 --> 00:25:15,540
The original braking system that was used on London Underground was designed by George

380
00:25:15,541 --> 00:25:18,086
Westinghouse of America in the 1870s.

381
00:25:18,087 --> 00:25:22,420
It consisted basically of compressed air supply, which provided

382
00:25:22,421 --> 00:25:28,820
you with a constant supply of air, and the air was to use in the brake cylinders, and

383
00:25:28,821 --> 00:25:34,100
the brake cylinders were used to push a block against the wheel to slow it down during braking.

384
00:25:34,101 --> 00:25:39,320
on each car a thing called a triple valve and the triple valve recharged the brakes,

385
00:25:39,321 --> 00:25:43,640
released the brakes and applied the brakes so it had three functions.

386
00:25:43,641 --> 00:25:48,900
Technical expertise and simplicity were the watchwords of the London Underground, a simplicity

387
00:25:48,901 --> 00:25:53,760
exemplified by Frank Pick and his hand-chosen architects.

388
00:25:53,761 --> 00:25:59,180
In 1923 Pick employed Charles Henry Holden to design the stations on the City and South

389
00:25:59,181 --> 00:26:00,992
London Railway extension.

390
00:26:00,993 --> 00:26:05,340
Holden went on to create a house style that rationalised the

391
00:26:05,341 --> 00:26:11,060
often chaotic layout and provided a much more modern facade, still to be seen in many underground

392
00:26:11,061 --> 00:26:12,060
stations.

393
00:26:12,061 --> 00:26:18,300
The design was a very clever one, based on simple geometric shapes and using a modern

394
00:26:18,301 --> 00:26:24,920
material and concrete for the roofs, but using traditional brickwork designs for the main

395
00:26:24,921 --> 00:26:31,700
body of the building and creating what now in retrospect is some of the best modern building

396
00:26:31,701 --> 00:26:36,040
designs in London and perhaps in the rest of Britain as well.

397
00:26:36,640 --> 00:26:40,469
But in one key area the underground was far from simple.

398
00:26:40,470 --> 00:26:42,520
As the network expanded it was

399
00:26:42,521 --> 00:26:46,820
becoming increasingly difficult to follow and understand the London Underground map

400
00:26:46,821 --> 00:26:48,207
or route planner.

401
00:26:48,208 --> 00:26:53,920
An alternative was needed and so in 1931 draftsman Harry Beck produced

402
00:26:53,921 --> 00:26:55,540
the first diagrammatic map.

403
00:26:55,541 --> 00:26:59,380
It was a complicated endeavour, illustrating the different lines

404
00:26:59,381 --> 00:27:02,966
and routes of the Underground. His solution was radical.

405
00:27:02,967 --> 00:27:05,400
He redesigned the map as an electrical

406
00:27:05,401 --> 00:27:10,028
circuit, abandoning geographical truth for the sake of simplicity.

407
00:27:10,029 --> 00:27:11,360
The central area of

408
00:27:11,361 --> 00:27:16,120
the map, which contains the most complicated interchanges, are enlarged in relation to

409
00:27:16,121 --> 00:27:17,124
the outer areas.

410
00:27:17,125 --> 00:27:21,640
Initially, London Underground rejected it as a design because it was too

411
00:27:21,641 --> 00:27:25,484
revolutionary and because it had no bearing on the real geography.

412
00:27:25,485 --> 00:27:26,940
They eventually published

413
00:27:26,941 --> 00:27:27,781
it in 1933.

414
00:27:27,782 --> 00:27:33,440
It was greeted with enthusiasm by the public for being clear and simple to

415
00:27:33,441 --> 00:27:34,141
use.

416
00:27:34,142 --> 00:27:39,400
The diagrammatic map is now a standard means of representing transport systems all

417
00:27:39,401 --> 00:27:40,448
over the world.

418
00:27:40,449 --> 00:27:45,340
The longest escalator in use on the London Underground is at the Angel

419
00:27:45,341 --> 00:27:47,621
station on the Northern Line.

420
00:27:47,622 --> 00:27:52,340
It is 197 feet long and has a vertical rise of 90 feet. This

421
00:27:52,380 --> 00:27:59,980
makes it the longest escalator in Europe.

422
00:27:59,981 --> 00:28:04,580
By the 1930s, control of all the original tube companies was taken over by the newly

423
00:28:04,581 --> 00:28:07,118
formed London Passenger Transport Board.

424
00:28:07,119 --> 00:28:10,100
Its role was to oversee the continued expansion

425
00:28:10,101 --> 00:28:11,249
of the underground.

426
00:28:11,250 --> 00:28:15,180
With the population of Greater London approaching 9 million, some

427
00:28:15,181 --> 00:28:19,680
had to be done to increase the flow of passengers through the system. It wasn't

428
00:28:19,681 --> 00:28:25,320
feasible to increase the tunnel size for wider trains, so in 1935 the cars were

429
00:28:25,321 --> 00:28:30,580
redesigned to accommodate more people. One of the major problems of all the

430
00:28:30,581 --> 00:28:35,640
pre-1935 trains built for London Underground was that you had the switch

431
00:28:35,641 --> 00:28:40,980
compartment behind the driver and that used up 15% of the available passenger

432
00:28:40,981 --> 00:28:41,681
space.

433
00:28:41,682 --> 00:28:48,300
By 1935 they had sufficient design progress to put most of the equipment below

434
00:28:48,301 --> 00:28:49,001
the floor.

435
00:28:49,002 --> 00:28:54,420
Motors were smaller, the compressors were smaller, all the power supply systems

436
00:28:54,421 --> 00:29:00,140
were smaller, and they produced these prototype trains in 1935.

437
00:29:00,141 --> 00:29:06,700
Since its opening in 1863, the London Underground has played a vital role in the lives of Londoners.

438
00:29:06,701 --> 00:29:11,700
But it would be during the dark days of World War II that the underground really shone through.

439
00:29:12,700 --> 00:29:17,700
In 1940, Germany unleashed a deadly bombing campaign on Britain, the Blitz.

440
00:29:17,701 --> 00:29:23,700
79 tube stations were used as air raid shelters accommodating thousands of people every night.

441
00:29:27,700 --> 00:29:32,700
The 130,000 people who sleep every night in tube stations raise a good many problems.

442
00:29:32,701 --> 00:29:35,960
They get there early and so need something to eat long before bedtime.

443
00:29:36,840 --> 00:29:40,200
London Transport has established six depots where the food is received

444
00:29:40,201 --> 00:29:42,580
and prepared for dispatch to the station canteens.

445
00:29:44,020 --> 00:29:47,380
The trains carried the women of the Underground Catering Service

446
00:29:47,381 --> 00:29:50,080
that provided refreshments for the thousands of Londoners

447
00:29:50,081 --> 00:29:53,800
who sheltered on the station platforms on countless nights during the war.

448
00:29:54,580 --> 00:30:01,120
The London Underground had one of the most efficient catering establishments.

449
00:30:01,121 --> 00:30:07,760
In fact, there was a total of over 75 which the catering department looked after.

450
00:30:08,180 --> 00:30:14,240
And so they took on the job of providing refreshment trains.

451
00:30:14,940 --> 00:30:16,540
Buns, chocolates, timetables.

452
00:30:17,180 --> 00:30:21,820
Now, these refreshment trains ran roughly about twice a day,

453
00:30:21,960 --> 00:30:27,640
once late evening and once before the service started in the morning.

454
00:30:27,641 --> 00:30:32,520
Tons of food are consumed every night. To prepare and deliver it is the work of over

455
00:30:32,521 --> 00:30:34,800
a thousand people.

456
00:30:34,801 --> 00:30:39,560
As well as looking after the needs of the people sheltering in the tube, 17,000 women

457
00:30:39,561 --> 00:30:41,567
took on jobs in the underground.

458
00:30:41,568 --> 00:30:45,080
They created a thriving underground world, producing the

459
00:30:45,081 --> 00:30:51,080
weapons of war in the munitions and armaments factories beneath the city's streets.

460
00:30:51,081 --> 00:30:55,240
During the Second World War, large numbers of women came to be employed by the underground

461
00:30:55,241 --> 00:30:59,640
group, mostly in place of men, of course, who had gone off to fight in the armed forces.

462
00:31:00,120 --> 00:31:05,260
But they took on a variety of roles, not just working in stations, but also in engineering

463
00:31:05,261 --> 00:31:10,760
capacities, such as relaying track or working in the underground factories, building new

464
00:31:10,761 --> 00:31:12,680
rolling stock and repairing trains.

465
00:31:13,680 --> 00:31:22,560
Millions of vital bomber, fighter and tank parts poured out from this underground arsenal

466
00:31:22,561 --> 00:31:27,220
to the Allied forces in all corners of the world.

467
00:31:28,840 --> 00:31:33,460
The underground was also important for the wartime Prime Minister, Winston Churchill.

468
00:31:34,060 --> 00:31:36,360
The deep tunnels beneath Down Street Station

469
00:31:36,361 --> 00:31:39,560
became the subterranean headquarters for the War Cabinet.

470
00:31:40,660 --> 00:31:45,500
This passageway would have been the way out of the station up until 1932,

471
00:31:45,920 --> 00:31:49,020
but it had a much more important role during the war years.

472
00:31:49,021 --> 00:31:54,100
The Railway Executive Committee used this area for their meeting room.

473
00:31:54,720 --> 00:32:01,180
It's where some of Britain's most important decisions for rail movements, troop movements during the war years were taken.

474
00:32:01,760 --> 00:32:04,100
But it also had another important use.

475
00:32:04,420 --> 00:32:14,400
Up until 1942, when the War Cabinet rooms were completed, Winston Churchill came here and met with some of the Joint Chiefs of Staff and with the War Cabinet.

476
00:32:14,401 --> 00:32:15,101
it.

477
00:32:15,102 --> 00:32:22,580
Here at track level, the platform areas were converted for use as a telephone exchange,

478
00:32:23,060 --> 00:32:28,900
as kitchens, as bedrooms and offices, and what we're standing in was one of the dining

479
00:32:28,901 --> 00:32:29,601
rooms.

480
00:32:29,602 --> 00:32:34,560
There were two dining rooms here, and we can tell that this one was one of the executive's

481
00:32:34,561 --> 00:32:40,120
dining rooms, and that was because they had wallpaper on the walls and a bell push to

482
00:32:40,121 --> 00:32:41,060
summon the steward.

483
00:32:44,400 --> 00:32:49,400
That gives you an idea of what kind of noise must have been present

484
00:32:49,401 --> 00:32:53,400
while people were trying to work, eat and even sleep

485
00:32:53,401 --> 00:32:58,400
as trains were passing by the site not more than six feet away from where we're standing.

486
00:32:59,400 --> 00:33:05,400
Between August 1940 and July 1941, nearly 40,000 high-explosive bombs

487
00:33:05,401 --> 00:33:08,400
and millions of incendiaries fell on London.

488
00:33:08,401 --> 00:33:12,400
Thousands of underground carriages were damaged or destroyed.

489
00:33:12,401 --> 00:33:16,400
But worse than the damage to the rolling stock and infrastructure of the underground,

490
00:33:16,401 --> 00:33:23,400
several disasters befell the tube that affected all those people who used the tunnels and platforms as shelter.

491
00:33:23,401 --> 00:33:29,400
Probably one of the worst was at Bank Station, which is right in the heart of Financial London,

492
00:33:29,401 --> 00:33:35,400
where a German bomb penetrated the ticket office roof and slid down the escalator shafts before exploding.

493
00:33:35,401 --> 00:33:40,400
And it took over 18 months to reconstruct the station from the devastation caused.

494
00:33:40,401 --> 00:33:43,400
56 people were killed in this disaster.

495
00:33:43,401 --> 00:33:48,400
In 1940, South London's Ballam Station was also destroyed by a German bomb

496
00:33:48,401 --> 00:33:52,400
which burst a water main drowning 68 people who were sheltering there.

497
00:33:52,401 --> 00:33:55,400
One of the worst civilian disasters during the Second World War

498
00:33:55,401 --> 00:33:58,400
took place at a partially built underground station,

499
00:33:58,401 --> 00:34:01,400
Bethnal Green in East London.

500
00:34:01,401 --> 00:34:03,400
Following a false air raid warning,

501
00:34:03,401 --> 00:34:07,400
a woman and child tripped halfway down the staircases

502
00:34:07,401 --> 00:34:11,400
and in the ensuing crush nearly 200 people were killed.

503
00:34:11,401 --> 00:34:17,400
This tragedy was so awful the British government kept the details secret for nearly two years.

504
00:34:18,400 --> 00:34:23,400
After the war, the underground required an enormous quantity of new rolling stock

505
00:34:23,401 --> 00:34:26,400
to replace the carriages and motorcars that had been destroyed.

506
00:34:26,401 --> 00:34:30,400
The new trains took to the tracks on the district line in 1953,

507
00:34:30,401 --> 00:34:34,400
just as the last of the wooden cars were scrapped from the system.

508
00:34:34,401 --> 00:34:37,900
They were inspired by wartime technology.

509
00:34:39,400 --> 00:34:42,600
Following the experience with aluminium aircraft construction,

510
00:34:43,420 --> 00:34:47,720
London Underground used that experience to develop an aluminium car body.

511
00:34:48,680 --> 00:34:52,260
Aluminium was a good material to use because it was 20% lighter than steel,

512
00:34:52,660 --> 00:34:55,860
it didn't rust, and you could also get away with not painting it.

513
00:34:56,740 --> 00:35:00,040
In fact, you could save two tonnes of paint per vehicle if you didn't paint it.

514
00:35:00,041 --> 00:35:06,680
So in 1953 they tried running an unpainted vehicle around the system and it was soon

515
00:35:06,681 --> 00:35:08,260
accepted by the public.

516
00:35:08,261 --> 00:35:14,420
So from that day onwards all London Underground trains were built of unpainted aluminium.

517
00:35:14,421 --> 00:35:20,440
And only since the mid-1980s when we were hit by the scourge of graffiti did London Underground

518
00:35:20,441 --> 00:35:24,560
start having to repaint vehicles.

519
00:35:24,561 --> 00:35:29,480
As the new aluminium trains came into service, London Transport ceased its steam passenger

520
00:35:29,481 --> 00:35:30,697
train operation.

521
00:35:30,698 --> 00:35:36,400
In 1961, after nearly a century, the steam locomotives found their way into

522
00:35:36,401 --> 00:35:39,197
museum collections and enthusiasts' clubs.

523
00:35:39,198 --> 00:35:42,460
For London Transport, the future lay in automatic

524
00:35:42,461 --> 00:35:48,040
trains. They began experimenting with them on the District Line in 1962.

525
00:35:48,041 --> 00:35:55,040
The world record for visiting all the 280 stations on the London Underground is 19 hours,

526
00:35:55,041 --> 00:35:57,207
59 minutes and 37 seconds.

527
00:35:57,208 --> 00:36:02,040
A team of six broke this underground record in March 2000.

528
00:36:05,040 --> 00:36:10,660
On March 7th 1969, London Underground again took the lead in the world when Her Majesty

529
00:36:10,661 --> 00:36:16,540
the Queen officially opened the Victoria Line, the first fully automated railway in the world.

530
00:36:16,541 --> 00:36:17,469
It was a pioneer.

531
00:36:17,470 --> 00:36:21,460
And the way it worked basically was that the driver would close the doors

532
00:36:21,461 --> 00:36:26,140
at the station and press two start buttons and the train would accelerate and proceed

533
00:36:26,141 --> 00:36:29,760
to the next station, brake and stop in the right place without the driver touching any

534
00:36:29,761 --> 00:36:30,811
of the controls.

535
00:36:30,812 --> 00:36:35,280
Each section of line was divided up into blocks a few hundred metres

536
00:36:35,281 --> 00:36:41,280
long and each block had a signal protecting it on the basis that you never allowed more

537
00:36:41,281 --> 00:36:44,013
than one train into any one block at any one time.

538
00:36:44,014 --> 00:36:45,980
If you automate it, what you have to

539
00:36:45,981 --> 00:36:51,240
do is to tell the train whether the block ahead is safe to go into. And they do that

540
00:36:51,241 --> 00:36:55,800
by transmitting a code through the rails, running rails,

541
00:36:55,801 --> 00:36:58,280
and this code is picked up by two receivers

542
00:36:58,281 --> 00:37:00,180
on the front of the train.

543
00:37:00,181 --> 00:37:02,160
And those receivers basically tell the train

544
00:37:02,161 --> 00:37:04,160
whether it's safe to go and at what speed.

545
00:37:05,180 --> 00:37:08,260
The Victoria Line, with its new age 67 stock,

546
00:37:08,261 --> 00:37:11,620
was the most modern urban railway in the world.

547
00:37:11,621 --> 00:37:13,240
The busiest section of the line,

548
00:37:13,241 --> 00:37:15,840
between Victoria Station and Buckingham Palace,

549
00:37:15,841 --> 00:37:19,420
carries 15,000 passengers during the rush hour.

550
00:37:19,421 --> 00:37:23,920
With the opening of the Victoria Line, automatic fare collection was introduced to make the

551
00:37:23,921 --> 00:37:25,860
underground more efficient.

552
00:37:25,861 --> 00:37:30,000
Passengers would purchase their tickets from machines instead of a ticket office.

553
00:37:30,001 --> 00:37:34,220
Their tickets were marked with a magnetically encoded strip that could be inserted into

554
00:37:34,221 --> 00:37:40,100
an unmanned barrier machine, giving them access to the platforms and the trains.

555
00:37:40,101 --> 00:37:42,600
This was an important time for the underground.

556
00:37:42,601 --> 00:37:47,480
After relying on the same equipment and stations for the last 50 years, they were in dire need

557
00:37:47,481 --> 00:37:48,920
of refurbishment.

558
00:37:48,921 --> 00:37:53,320
So in the early 1980s, many of the stations received a complete facelift.

559
00:37:53,960 --> 00:37:58,860
Expansions and renovations took place, which gave the central line a completely new look.

560
00:38:00,280 --> 00:38:04,180
The system runs daily, from 4.30 in the morning till midnight,

561
00:38:04,580 --> 00:38:07,060
when it shuts down for cleaning and daily maintenance.

562
00:38:07,640 --> 00:38:10,060
The staff who make this possible are rarely seen.

563
00:38:10,620 --> 00:38:14,500
They are the maintenance gangs who work at night when the track current is switched off.

564
00:38:15,220 --> 00:38:20,040
Some parts of the track are over 100 years old and require constant safety checks.

565
00:38:20,600 --> 00:38:24,420
Every bit of the line is walked every 48 hours to check for faults.

566
00:38:25,080 --> 00:38:29,660
But the age of the system was to be responsible for the London Underground's most recent tragedy.

567
00:38:30,540 --> 00:38:37,020
On Wednesday evening, November 18th, 1987, a small fire started under an escalator at King's Cross.

568
00:38:37,460 --> 00:38:42,380
The fire spread very rapidly up the escalator and right across the ticket hall.

569
00:38:42,381 --> 00:38:46,100
It's believed to have been started by a smoker dropping a match

570
00:38:46,101 --> 00:38:48,420
down the side of the treads on the escalator.

571
00:38:49,220 --> 00:38:52,080
It spread so rapidly that 31 people died in the fire,

572
00:38:52,340 --> 00:38:54,260
including an officer from the London Fire Brigade,

573
00:38:54,640 --> 00:38:56,280
and many more were seriously injured.

574
00:38:56,900 --> 00:38:59,180
London Underground itself did a great many changes

575
00:38:59,181 --> 00:39:00,840
immediately after King's Cross.

576
00:39:01,600 --> 00:39:04,440
There's a very strong no-smoking policy

577
00:39:04,441 --> 00:39:05,860
within the whole of London Underground.

578
00:39:07,400 --> 00:39:10,200
After King's Cross, the Underground went through a period

579
00:39:10,201 --> 00:39:13,200
of safety improvement and modernization.

580
00:39:13,201 --> 00:39:15,960
This improved Londoner's perception of the Tube.

581
00:39:15,961 --> 00:39:18,620
It was also voted the third most impressive attraction

582
00:39:18,621 --> 00:39:21,680
in London by millions of visiting tourists.

583
00:39:21,681 --> 00:39:23,380
But for nine million Londoners,

584
00:39:23,381 --> 00:39:25,940
it is just a way of getting to work.

585
00:39:25,941 --> 00:39:29,160
The experience of the rush hour is never a pleasant one.

586
00:39:29,161 --> 00:39:31,100
During the morning and afternoon peak hours,

587
00:39:31,101 --> 00:39:34,820
210,000 passengers passed through Oxford Circus,

588
00:39:34,821 --> 00:39:39,900
and 240,000 use Victoria on route to and from work.

589
00:39:39,901 --> 00:39:45,140
For the 1990s, London Underground has introduced three new types of trains on three of the lines.

590
00:39:45,860 --> 00:39:51,660
1992 on the Central Line and 1995 on the Northern, 1996 on the Jubilee.

591
00:39:51,661 --> 00:39:57,120
Their traction system uses what we call electronic power.

592
00:39:58,460 --> 00:40:02,820
And the electronic power means that you can control the train with computers.

593
00:40:03,840 --> 00:40:08,660
The old direct current motors have been replaced by what we call automating current motors.

594
00:40:08,661 --> 00:40:16,300
The reason for using these new motors is that they do not require the mechanical parts of the old DC motors,

595
00:40:16,660 --> 00:40:19,940
particularly brushes and the extra windings of the armature.

596
00:40:20,780 --> 00:40:24,780
This reduces the maintenance liability and improves the performance of the motors.

597
00:40:26,020 --> 00:40:28,880
And that's what's now standard on electric railways across the world.

598
00:40:29,880 --> 00:40:33,260
The Tube is a constantly expanding railway network.

599
00:40:33,560 --> 00:40:37,660
From its opening in 1979 by His Royal Highness the Prince of Wales,

600
00:40:37,661 --> 00:40:43,580
the Jubilee Line, the latest line in London, has developed into a modern, state-of-the-art railway.

601
00:40:52,420 --> 00:40:57,140
The Jubilee Line extension has taken 30,000 people six years to build.

602
00:40:59,880 --> 00:41:04,080
The additional 10 miles of tunnels cross the River Thames on four occasions,

603
00:41:04,081 --> 00:41:10,920
and 12 new stations are now in operation on what is one of the most high-tech and advanced underground railways in the world.

604
00:41:11,760 --> 00:41:16,080
Architects, Foster and Partners created the space-age look of Canary Wharf Station.

605
00:41:17,060 --> 00:41:23,720
With projects such as these, there really is an interreaction between the architecture and the engineering of the stations.

606
00:41:23,920 --> 00:41:26,340
They're very complex building projects.

607
00:41:26,880 --> 00:41:31,320
Canary Wharf was in a dock that had to be drained and then excavated still further,

608
00:41:31,321 --> 00:41:33,880
so they're very much below water level.

609
00:41:34,080 --> 00:41:39,660
So if you imagine the building being effectively floating in water,

610
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:43,460
held down to the gravel levels below,

611
00:41:44,100 --> 00:41:46,800
every time you build another massive building next to it,

612
00:41:46,880 --> 00:41:49,420
it causes movement within the overall box.

613
00:41:49,421 --> 00:41:51,760
So the planning of that and the thinking of that

614
00:41:51,761 --> 00:41:55,920
from an architectural point of view and also from an engineering point of view

615
00:41:55,921 --> 00:41:57,240
were very complex.

616
00:41:58,560 --> 00:42:01,800
The Jubilee Line Extension employs the latest technology

617
00:42:01,801 --> 00:42:04,420
to deliver an efficient and safe railway system.

618
00:42:05,300 --> 00:42:07,740
Safety is paramount, and this totally new line

619
00:42:07,741 --> 00:42:10,720
can run 56 trains at any one time on the system.

620
00:42:11,360 --> 00:42:13,560
The trains are all new and are all controlled

621
00:42:13,561 --> 00:42:15,340
from their state-of-the-art nerve centre.

622
00:42:16,260 --> 00:42:19,360
Radiolinks enable the centre to be in contact with every driver,

623
00:42:19,860 --> 00:42:21,500
and onboard information systems

624
00:42:21,501 --> 00:42:24,100
keeps passengers aware of changing travel details.

625
00:42:27,180 --> 00:42:29,720
In the future, London Underground will bring us

626
00:42:29,721 --> 00:42:31,500
bigger and fully automated trains,

627
00:42:31,800 --> 00:42:35,000
With nearly three million people using the tube every day,

628
00:42:35,280 --> 00:42:37,200
the system will need to be more efficient.

629
00:42:38,840 --> 00:42:41,400
I think Londoners want an underground system

630
00:42:41,401 --> 00:42:44,840
which enables them to travel to more places in more comfort.

631
00:42:46,240 --> 00:42:50,560
Tourists do seem to be remarkably keen on the underground

632
00:42:50,561 --> 00:42:51,960
than native Londoners,

633
00:42:52,140 --> 00:42:54,240
probably because they don't have to use it every day.

634
00:42:54,780 --> 00:42:57,120
I think more than anything, though,

635
00:42:57,121 --> 00:43:01,660
people want to be able to feel that they can sit in newer rolling,

636
00:43:01,800 --> 00:43:06,160
stock and I say sit rather than stand they'd like a seat so therefore they'd

637
00:43:06,161 --> 00:43:12,360
like more trains coming more quickly I think most people think that London is

638
00:43:12,361 --> 00:43:17,100
one thing and the London Underground is another but in fact the two things are

639
00:43:17,101 --> 00:43:22,380
intermingled in a way the Underground is London it's the only thing holding

640
00:43:22,381 --> 00:43:27,180
London together plans now exist for passenger capacity to be increased by

641
00:43:27,181 --> 00:43:30,960
making better use of the available tunnel diameter lowering the floor of

642
00:43:30,961 --> 00:43:36,060
carriage and providing more gangway space for standing. This concept has been called

643
00:43:36,061 --> 00:43:41,780
space train and gaps would be eliminated between cars by using short articulated segments to

644
00:43:41,781 --> 00:43:45,581
provide one continuous train instead of individual cars.

645
00:43:45,582 --> 00:43:48,160
It is hoped that the space train would

646
00:43:48,161 --> 00:43:53,820
transport 60,000 passengers per hour along the Victoria Line.

647
00:43:53,821 --> 00:43:56,628
The underground as we know it is just about full.

648
00:43:56,629 --> 00:43:58,920
There is no such thing as London without

649
00:43:58,921 --> 00:43:59,755
the Tube.

650
00:43:59,756 --> 00:44:07,180
Of the one million people that came to work this morning, only 15% came in cars.

651
00:44:07,840 --> 00:44:10,184
85% came by public transport.

652
00:44:10,185 --> 00:44:15,600
Half of them used the underground for part or all of their journey.

653
00:44:15,601 --> 00:44:19,390
It was as the Tube grew that London grew.

654
00:44:19,391 --> 00:44:24,660
The railway system is what makes London live and breathe.

655
00:44:24,661 --> 00:44:33,660
137 years ago steam trains carried passengers beneath the streets of London on the world's first underground railway.

656
00:44:33,661 --> 00:44:41,660
Electric traction and American financing modernised and expanded the system, taking the tube lines deeper beneath the city's streets.

657
00:44:41,661 --> 00:44:50,660
As London's population has grown and her traffic problems increased, the tube has become indispensable for keeping the city moving.

658
00:44:50,661 --> 00:44:55,620
It is now recognized across the world as a symbol of London itself.

659
00:44:56,440 --> 00:44:59,760
The future promises faster, larger trains and line extensions.

660
00:45:00,640 --> 00:45:03,280
It may be the oldest underground system in the world,

661
00:45:03,780 --> 00:45:10,460
but the London Underground is ready to provide the city with all her transport needs beyond the 21st century.

